  "reconstructed_text": "For every three real numbers r,h,c satisfying r≥0, h>0, and h≤c, define a=(h+c)/(1+r), r_p=r+a⁻¹, A_plus=r_p⁻¹, and ρ=2c/(1+2c). These are local abbreviations, not additional hypotheses. Then r_p>0, A_plus>0, A_plus≤2c, ρ>0, and ρ<1. In addition, if r>0, then A_plus≤ρ·r⁻¹. All variables, products, inequalities, reciprocals, and divisions are real. The reciprocal convention is totalized, with 0⁻¹=0. The stated assumptions give c>0 and make 1+r and 1+2c positive; they also make a positive, while the first conclusion gives positivity of r_p. Their reciprocals therefore have their ordinary nonzero-denominator meanings. The premise r≥0 allows r=0. At that boundary, all five unconditional conclusions still apply, but the final implication imposes no bound involving r⁻¹ because its separate premise r>0 is false. In particular, one must not extend A_plus≤ρ·r⁻¹ to r=0: its totalized right-hand side would be zero, whereas A_plus is strictly positive. No further iterative, asymptotic, or probabilistic conclusion is asserted.",
